;; What turns "WireGuard is installed" into "this is a VPN appliance".
;;
;; A box nobody can log into that also does nothing is a brick with good
;; provenance. This pack makes the appliance alive on first boot: it formats
;; its state partition if it is blank, generates a keypair if there is none,
;; brings up wg0, and prints the public key on the console. There are no
;; peers until someone adds one, which is the only part a deployment has to
;; supply.
;;
;; It also masks the login prompt, which profile:locked could not do on its
;; own. BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_GETTY=n governs Buildroot's own getty setup; under
;; systemd the console login comes from serial-getty@.service, generated from
;; systemd's presets and the console named on the kernel command line, and no
;; Kconfig symbol reaches it. A boot found that: the password was locked, so
;; login failed with "bad salt", but the prompt was still there. Masking is a
;; symlink to /dev/null, which is systemd's own mechanism for "this unit can
;; never start".

(pack wireguard-appliance
  (version "0.1.0")
  (doc "First-boot provisioning for a WireGuard appliance, and no console login")

  (requires
    (buildroot "2025.02.16"))

  (external "br2-external")

  (provides
    (feature wireguard-appliance)))
